1. In WebGPURenderer.readPixels (src/engine2d/gpu/WebGPURenderer.ts:465-469), remove the `if (a > 0 && a < 255) { ... }` block. It should only swizzle BGRA to RGBA and copy the premultiplied bytes as-is, matching Pixi 8.17 WebGL getPixels. Update the doc comment, which currently says 未预乘, to say the output is premultiplied.
2. In createExtract's base64, apply Pixi's defaults and mapping:
```ts
const IMAGE_TYPES = { png: 'image/png', jpg: 'image/jpeg', webp: 'image/webp' } as const;
const format = opts?.format ?? 'png';
const quality = opts?.quality ?? 1;
return c.toDataURL(IMAGE_TYPES[format], quality);
```

Keep a failed pipeline to its own draws, as in master. All changes are in src/rendering/rhi/backends/luma/LumaRhiDevice.ts.

(1) Add a `failed` flag to LumaRhiRenderPipeline and LumaRhiComputePipeline.

(2) Catch validation and internal errors even though luma has debug off. In `createShader` and `createRenderPipeline`, get the native device with `const gpu = (this.luma as unknown as {handle: GPUDevice}).handle`. Around the `this.luma.createShader(...)` call and around the `this.luma.createRenderPipeline(...)` call:
- call `gpu.pushErrorScope('validation')` and `gpu.pushErrorScope('internal')`;
- after the call, pop both scopes;
- if either pop returns an error, set the pipeline's `failed = true`, reject `ready` with that error, and report it once through `report`.
For the pipeline, feed these popErrorScope promises into `waitPipelineReady` instead of relying on `linkStatus`.

(3) When a shader's compilationStatus is 'error', set `failed = true` in the existing `ready.catch` handler.

(4) In LumaRenderPassEncoder:
- in `setPipeline`, if `p.failed`, set `this.pipeline = p` and `this.skipping = true`, and return without calling `this.pass.setPipeline`;
- make `setBindings`, `draw` and `drawIndexed` no-ops while `skipping` is set;
- in those draws, increment `stats.skippedDraws` and call `device._warnSkippedDraw(p)` once per pipeline;
- clear `skipping` on the next `setPipeline` with a valid pipeline.

Skipping `setBindings` as well matters. A bind group built from an invalid pipeline's layout is itself invalid and would also invalidate the pass.

Do the same for compute passes (`setPipeline` and `dispatch`). A pipeline whose state is still unknown (error scope not popped yet) can keep drawing as today, which keeps behaviour unchanged for valid shaders. Add a unit test with a fake luma device whose error scope reports an error: the frame must still submit the other draws, and skippedDraws must be 1.

In LumaRhiDevice.destroy() (src/rendering/rhi/backends/luma/LumaRhiDevice.ts, just before `this.luma.destroy();`), tear down the canvas context. This call destroys the framebuffer and depth attachment, unconfigures the GPUCanvasContext, and stops the Resize/Intersection observers and the DPR matchMedia listener:
```ts
try { this.luma.getDefaultCanvasContext().destroy(); } catch (e) { this.report(e, 'warning'); }
this.luma.destroy();
```
Use the report severity that fits the RHI diagnostics enum, or an empty catch. Add a regression test modelled on tmp/review/verify-rhi-6/destroy.test.ts. It should assert that after rhi.destroy() no ResizeObserver, IntersectionObserver or matchMedia listener is still live and that unconfigure() was called.

In GpuTextures, register the source listeners once per source instead of every time an entry is created. Add `private readonly hooked = new WeakSet<TextureSource>();` and replace lines 77-78 with:

```ts
if (!this.hooked.has(source)) {
  this.hooked.add(source);
  source.once('destroy', () => this.release(source));
  source.on('unload', () => this.release(source));
}
```

The unload listener has to stay registered across cycles, because the entry is recreated on the next get(). TextureSource.destroy() already calls removeAllListeners() (TextureSource.ts:162), so nothing leaks after destroy. This keeps the listener count constant, as Pixi's managed-hash guard does. A regression test is to assert that listenerCount('unload') is still 1 after several render/unload and RenderTexture.resize/render cycles.
